---
import Carousel from "../components/Carousel.astro";
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import BrandMark from "../components/BrandMark.astro";
---

<MainLayout>

<div id="top"></div>

<Header />

<main id="main-content">
<section class="home-about" id="about" aria-labelledby="about-title">
<Hero />

<div class="about">
  <div class="about-intro">
    <span class="eyebrow">ABOUT &amp; LEADERSHIP APPROACH</span>
    <h2 id="about-title">From technical operations to enterprise transformation</h2>
    <p>
      Across 17+ years, I have progressed from database and technical operations into senior program leadership. I solve the operating problems between strategy and execution: unclear ownership, fragmented reporting, late dependencies, inconsistent delivery controls and automation without accountability.
    </p>
    <p class="leadership-principle"><strong>Leadership principle:</strong> make the decision visible, name the owner, establish the evidence and design the control so teams can sustain it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="capability-grid">
    <article class="capability-card"><h3>Enterprise Program Leadership</h3><p>Translate executive intent into governed portfolios, dependency decisions, release confidence and measurable outcomes.</p></article>
    <article class="capability-card"><h3>Cloud &amp; DevOps Operations</h3><p>Connect CloudOps, DevOps, platform reliability, service performance and release governance in one operating model.</p></article>
    <article class="capability-card"><h3>Delivery &amp; Jira Governance</h3><p>Standardize commitment, scope, readiness, workflow quality and executive visibility without adding process theatre.</p></article>
    <article class="capability-card"><h3>Responsible AI Operations</h3><p>Use automation to reduce reporting noise and prompt action while preserving human ownership, escalation and auditability.</p></article>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="impact" id="impact" aria-labelledby="impact-title">

<div class="section-heading">
<span class="eyebrow">DELIVERY IMPACT</span>
<h2 id="impact-title">I make complex delivery governable</h2>
<p>Senior program leadership grounded in operating evidence, accountable decisions and sustainable execution.</p>
</div>

<Carousel
  id="impact"
  label="Delivery impact carousel"
  positionLabel="outcomes"
  cssOffsetVar="--impact-offset"
  cardSelector=".impact-card"
  total={19}
>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Global Teams <span>10</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Led 10 globally distributed delivery teams.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Budget Efficiency <span>20%</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Reduced program costs by 20% through governance.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Cloud Migrations <span>15</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Directed 15 enterprise-scale cloud transformations.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Risk Reduction <span>30%</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Cut delivery risks by 30% via proactive frameworks.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Agile Adoption <span>25</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Rolled out agile practices across 25 programs.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Stakeholder Alignment <span>95%</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Achieved 95% satisfaction in executive steering.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Delivery Velocity <span>40%</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Improved release cycle speed by 40%.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Compliance Audits <span>100%</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Passed 100% of external audits with zero findings.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Cross-Functional Teams <span>50+</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Coordinated 50+ teams across IT, Ops, and Business.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Delivery Success <span>98%</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Achieved 98% on-time program delivery.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Cost Savings <span>$50M</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Drove $50M savings via optimization.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Process Automation <span>200+</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Implemented 200+ automated workflows.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Vendor Management <span>30</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Governed 30 strategic vendor partnerships.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Change Adoption <span>85%</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Secured 85% user adoption in transformations.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Innovation Programs <span>12</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Launched 12 enterprise innovation initiatives.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Employee Engagement <span>90%</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Boosted engagement scores to 90%.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Portfolio Scale <span>100+</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Oversaw 100+ concurrent projects.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Escalation Reduction <span>40%</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Reduced executive escalations by 40%.</p></article>
  <article class="impact-card"><strong>Digital KPIs <span>25</span></strong><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><p>Defined and tracked 25 digital success metrics.</p></article>
</Carousel>

</section>

<section class="career" aria-labelledby="career-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <span class="eyebrow">CAREER PROGRESSION</span>
    <h2 id="career-title">A career built across the full operating stack</h2>
    <p>Each step added a wider leadership surface—from technical reliability to enterprise portfolio decisions.</p>
  </div>
  <ol class="career-timeline">
    <li><span>2007–2009</span><strong>Capgemini</strong><p>Built the database and production-operations foundation for disciplined reliability and incident response.</p></li>
    <li><span>2011–2021</span><strong>Cognizant</strong><p>Expanded into enterprise operations and people leadership, connecting service delivery with measurable operational control.</p></li>
    <li><span>2021</span><strong>Mindtree</strong><p>Led service-delivery management with sharper governance, customer accountability and execution visibility.</p></li>
    <li><span>2021–2022</span><strong>Deep LogicTech</strong><p>Moved into program leadership, coordinating cross-functional delivery and establishing repeatable management rhythms.</p></li>
    <li><span>2023–Present</span><strong>Conga</strong><p>Leads enterprise operations, delivery governance, executive reporting and responsible automation transformations.</p></li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="stories" id="success-stories" aria-labelledby="stories-title">
  <div class="section-heading stories-heading">
    <span class="eyebrow">PROVEN OUTCOMES</span>
    <h2 id="stories-title">Transformation made measurable</h2>
    <p>Three evidence-based cases showing the context, personal mandate, interventions, controls and leadership value.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="story-grid">
    <article class="story-card story-featured">
      <div class="story-meta"><span>Cloud Operations SLA Transformation</span><strong>+22%</strong></div>
      <h3>Turned SLA reporting into preventive operational control</h3>
      <p><strong>Context:</strong> aggregate SLA reporting masked aging work, incorrect priorities and tickets left in active states after resolution.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>My mandate:</strong> create ownership, early-warning signals and a consistent leadership review.</li>
        <li><strong>Intervention:</strong> combined amber, breach, oldest-open, priority and workflow-quality controls.</li>
        <li><strong>Outcome:</strong> improved SLA adherence by 22% and moved reviews from retrospective reporting to early action.</li>
        <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> Jira/JSM SLA measures, scheduled health reporting and governed exception reviews.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="story-card">
      <div class="story-meta"><span>Global Delivery Governance</span><strong>Enterprise scale</strong></div>
      <h3>Created one commitment language across distributed teams</h3>
      <p>Established a shared commitment language across globally distributed teams, using unified sprint cadence, DoR/DoD, dependency visibility, scope control, rollover governance and release-readiness evidence.</p>
      <p class="story-proof"><strong>Leadership example:</strong> exceptions became the agenda—what changed, who owns recovery and which decision protects the release.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="story-card">
      <div class="story-meta"><span>Responsible AI for Program Operations</span><strong>Human-led</strong></div>
      <h3>Designed automation that earns operational trust</h3>
      <p>Combined automated executive reporting with context-aware sprint nudges, anti-spam controls, pause states, escalation and traceable human ownership.</p>
      <p class="story-proof"><strong>Control example:</strong> AI summarizes a verified signal and asks one useful question; it never changes priority, commitment or closure autonomously.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <p class="evidence-note"><strong>Evidence and confidentiality:</strong> the 22% improvement and five-team scope reflect professional delivery evidence. Operational examples are generalized to protect employer, customer, ticket, environment and financial information.</p>
</section>

<section class="frameworks" id="frameworks" aria-labelledby="frameworks-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <span class="eyebrow">PRACTICAL FRAMEWORKS</span>
    <h2 id="frameworks-title">Governance that accelerates delivery</h2>
    <p>Reusable operating models designed to create clarity, accountability and measurable flow.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="framework-carousel">
    <Carousel
      id="framework"
      label="Governance frameworks carousel"
      positionLabel="frameworks"
      cssOffsetVar="--framework-offset"
      cardSelector=".framework-card"
      total={6}
    >
      <article class="framework-card"><span>DELIVERY CONTROL SYSTEM</span><h3>Commitment → exception → decision</h3><dl><div><dt>Inputs</dt><dd>Priorities, capacity, dependencies, risks</dd></div><div><dt>Controls</dt><dd>DoR/DoD, scope baseline, readiness gates</dd></div><div><dt>Outputs</dt><dd>Confidence, recovery owner, decision</dd></div></dl><p><strong>Example:</strong> a dependency change becomes a named release decision before it becomes rollover.</p></article>
      <article class="framework-card"><span>RELEASE READINESS</span><h3>Evidence → gate → promotion</h3><dl><div><dt>Inputs</dt><dd>Scope, tests, dependencies, rollback</dd></div><div><dt>Controls</dt><dd>Acceptance gates, owner sign-off, environment proof</dd></div><div><dt>Outputs</dt><dd>Go/no-go decision and recovery path</dd></div></dl><p><strong>Example:</strong> a release moves only when committed scope and live-environment evidence reconcile.</p></article>
      <article class="framework-card"><span>OPERATIONAL HEALTH</span><h3>Signal → ownership → recovery</h3><dl><div><dt>Inputs</dt><dd>SLA, aging, incidents, cost signals</dd></div><div><dt>Controls</dt><dd>Amber windows, priority quality, escalation</dd></div><div><dt>Outputs</dt><dd>Recovery action, owner, closure evidence</dd></div></dl><p><strong>Example:</strong> an aging high-impact request is reviewed before the resolution SLA breaches.</p></article>
      <article class="framework-card"><span>DEPENDENCY GOVERNANCE</span><h3>Dependency → exposure → owner</h3><dl><div><dt>Inputs</dt><dd>Milestones, contracts, sequencing, external needs</dd></div><div><dt>Controls</dt><dd>Critical-path review, due dates, escalation thresholds</dd></div><div><dt>Outputs</dt><dd>Protected milestone and explicit trade-off</dd></div></dl><p><strong>Example:</strong> a cross-team dependency is resolved or deliberately re-sequenced before it affects a committed date.</p></article>
      <article class="framework-card"><span>EXECUTIVE DECISION RHYTHM</span><h3>Evidence → choice → accountability</h3><dl><div><dt>Inputs</dt><dd>Outcome health, exposure, options, impact</dd></div><div><dt>Controls</dt><dd>Decision log, owner, due date, closure evidence</dd></div><div><dt>Outputs</dt><dd>Faster intervention and traceable follow-through</dd></div></dl><p><strong>Example:</strong> a leadership review ends with named decisions, not another status-collection cycle.</p></article>
      <article class="framework-card"><span>RESPONSIBLE AI ADOPTION</span><h3>Use case → control → measurable value</h3><dl><div><dt>Inputs</dt><dd>Decision need, source quality, human owner</dd></div><div><dt>Controls</dt><dd>Audit trail, anti-spam, pause, escalation</dd></div><div><dt>Outputs</dt><dd>Adoption, time returned, better decisions</dd></div></dl><p><strong>Example:</strong> the sprint agent nudges once, respects pause states and escalates only unresolved risk.</p></article>
    </Carousel>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="articles" id="articles" aria-labelledby="articles-title">
  <div class="articles-intro">
    <span class="eyebrow">INSIGHTS &amp; FRAMEWORKS</span>
    <h2 id="articles-title">Insights and reporting frameworks</h2>
    <p>Each view combines a governed signal, accountable owner, required action and evidence of closure.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="article-list progressive-panel" aria-label="Article summaries">
    <details open><summary><span>Cost Governance</span><h3>The Day the Budget Lied</h3></summary><p>How accurate budget reporting can still be completely wrong — and what a cost truth dashboard actually looks like. Three structural lies that keep programs green until they aren't.</p><a class="article-read-link" href="/insights/the-day-the-budget-lied/">Read article →</a></details>
    <details><summary><span>AI for PMs</span><h3>The AI PM Credential Nobody Sells</h3></summary><p>The gap between you and the next generation of PM work is not knowledge about AI — it is judgment about when to trust it. A 30-60-90 day plan for building that judgment.</p><a class="article-read-link" href="/insights/the-ai-pm-credential-nobody-sells/">Read article →</a></details>
    <details><summary><span>AI for PMs</span><h3>Your AI Copilot Has No Skin in the Game</h3></summary><p>AI generates confidently. It does not own the outcome. A six-point pre-sprint checklist for every AI-assisted user story, acceptance criterion and technical decision.</p><a class="article-read-link" href="/insights/your-ai-copilot-has-no-skin-in-the-game/">Read article →</a></details>
    <details><summary><span>Executive Governance</span><h3>Why Your Executive Dashboard Is a Political Document</h3></summary><p>Most executive dashboards show mostly green. A five-dimension governance scorecard for distinguishing a dashboard that informs from one that protects.</p><a class="article-read-link" href="/insights/why-your-executive-dashboard-is-a-political-document/">Read article →</a></details>
    <details><summary><span>Financial Governance</span><h3>The Invisible Architecture of Cost Control</h3></summary><p>Cost-Based Structuring is the discipline nobody teaches. The programs that stay in budget are not the ones with better forecasters — they have a better architecture.</p><a class="article-read-link" href="/insights/the-invisible-architecture-of-cost-control/">Read article →</a></details>
    <details><summary><span>Delivery Governance</span><h3>SAFe Ceremonies Are Not Your Problem</h3></summary><p>Organisations that struggle with SAFe rarely struggle with the ceremonies. They struggle with the decisions the ceremonies are supposed to produce.</p><a class="article-read-link" href="/insights/safe-ceremonies-are-not-your-problem/">Read article →</a></details>
    <details><summary><span>Cost Architecture</span><h3>Nine Layers Down: The Real Architecture of Project Cost Management</h3></summary><p>Most programs fail on cost not because they overspend, but because they manage one layer when the problem lives three layers deeper. A layer health check for every program leader.</p><a class="article-read-link" href="/insights/nine-layers-down-project-cost-management/">Read article →</a></details>
  </div>
  <a class="all-insights-link" href="/insights/">View all insights →</a>
</section>

